Findings (Code.dll):
- The ability bar is `PartUnitUISettings.Slots` (saved per unit). Auto-fill (`TryToInitialize`, only when the AutofillActionbarSlots setting is on) skips weapon-sourced abilities, which only show in the weapon section and disappear with the weapon. So heroes need the abilities as **their own facts** (AddFacts in a party feature), not `AddAbilitiesToCurrentWeapon`.
- Greying out: `AbilityCasterHasWeaponWithRangeType: Ranged` (already on all three) is a caster restriction that shows the ability as unavailable. Check it reads the active weapon set.
- A character-owned ability has `AbilityData.Weapon == null`. `Range: Weapon` then resolves to no range (`RuleCalculateAbilityRange` → `GetWeaponStats(null)`), so postfix `RuleCalculateAbilityRange.OnTrigger` for our three abilities to use the caster''s current ranged weapon''s range. The cone resize (`OverwatchCone.ConeFor(.., ability.RangeCells)`) then caps correctly too. The cut Veteran overwatch was a character-owned Weapon-type ability, so the engine is meant to support this.
- `OverrideWeapon` is saved (JsonProperty); don''t use it to hold the current gun.

Plan:
- `Overwatch_PartyFeature.jbp`: hidden feature with AddFacts of the three abilities. `PartyGrant` grants it (and removes `Overwatch_Feature` from party members if an earlier build gave it). Enemies keep `Overwatch_Feature` (weapon slots; RTO-T11 AI relies on it).
- Bar placement in C# after granting (area load, recruit): place each ability whose slot isn''t already on the bar, honouring `m_RemovedFromActionBar` (if the player removed it on purpose, leave it off).
- Range postfix as above.

Acceptance: on load, every party member has the three buttons in slots 8–10 (or the next free row); they''re greyed with a melee weapon or two melee weapons and lit with a gun; swapping weapon sets updates them immediately; removing one from the bar keeps it removed.', '2026-10-26T20:59:51Z');

As a player, every hero (and enemy) holding a ranged weapon has an **Overwatch** button on the
weapon''s action bar. It works like a built-in ability: it follows the game''s rules, uses the
engine''s own overwatch mechanic, and has a matching icon. Best case it''s a cone you can lengthen or
shorten (Gears Tactics / Zero Company); fallback is XCOM 2 style overwatch.

