- `OwlcatModificationManifest.json` (UniqueName `RogueTrader.Overwatch`, Version, DisplayName, Description, Author).
- `OwlcatModificationSettings.json` with `BlueprintPatches: [{Guid, Filename, PatchType: 1}]` (1 = Edit → `<Filename>.jbp_patch`; 0 = Replace).
- `Blueprints/**/*.jbp` and `*.jbp_patch` as-is; `Localization/enGB.json` as `{"strings":[{"Key","Value"}]}`.
- Install to `%LocalAppData%Low\Owlcat Games\Warhammer 40000 Rogue Trader\Modifications\RogueTrader.Overwatch` and add the name to `EnabledModifications` in `OwlcatModificationManagerSettings.json` (currently empty).
Patch semantics (RogueTrader.GameCore `BlueprintPatcher`): `FieldOverrides` take dotted paths (`WeaponAbilities.Ability5.m_Ability`), `ComponentsPatches` add components, `ArrayPatches` edit reference arrays.
Acceptance: Shift+F10 lists the mod; game log (`Player.log`, `PFLog.Mods`) shows patches applied with no errors.', '2026-10-26T18:25:46Z');

Mechanism: `AddAbilitiesToCurrentWeapon` (TypeId b787865cc7284095914908b39df0a903; on BlueprintUnitFact or BlueprintUnit) adds extra `WeaponAbility` slots to the wielder''s active weapon, filtered by Category/Family/Classification/HoldingType. Ranged weapons are Category Pistol (131), Basic (269), Heavy (51); skip Thrown (grenades). So: one component per ranged category, each listing every Overwatch ability. Also put `AbilityCasterHasWeaponWithRangeType: Ranged` on each ability as a guard.

Delivery:
- Enemies: every non-player unit gets `BlueprintWarhammerRoot.CommonMobFact` (BaseUnitEntity.OnInitialize). ComponentsPatch the components straight onto that fact blueprint, so units in existing saves get them too.
- Heroes: no shared player fact exists. Options to test: patch the protagonist + companion + mercenary BlueprintUnits (component is AllowedOn BlueprintUnit), or an `AddFactsToParty` feature. Pick whichever covers custom mercenaries and existing saves.
Fallback if this misbehaves: FieldOverride slot 5 (`WeaponAbilities.Ability5`) on the 38 ranged weapon prototypes (slot 5 is filled on only 4 items).
Acceptance: a companion with a lasgun shows the buttons, swaps to a sword and they vanish; enemies with guns can use it.', '2026-10-26T18:25:49Z');

XCOM 2 overwatch: shoot the first enemy that moves or acts within range, once, then overwatch ends.

`AbilityEffectOverwatch` Mode `Overwatch` (PartOverwatch.TryTriggerAttack calls Stop() after the first shot in this mode), HitsPerTarget `HitOnce`, `UseFirstWeaponAbility: true` (the weapon''s single shot). Zone: a `Circle` BlueprintAttackPattern around the unit (radius ~ weapon range; start 10 cells), no aiming. `WarhammerEndTurn` with `clearMPInsteadOfEndingTurn: false` (ends turn, like XCOM). Base it on the live, unused `Weapons/Abilities/Overwatch_BasicAbility` (1d271e94…, 2 AP) but with our own GUIDs and strings.
Triggers are engine rules: OverwatchController fires on enemy movement or on abilities with `UsingInOverwatchArea: WillCauseAttack`; overwatch ends at the unit''s next turn or when it acts.', '2026-10-26T19:22:57Z');

## Rules
- Press the button, then move the cursor: the cone resizes live. A farther cursor gives a longer, narrower cone; a closer one gives a shorter, wider cone, capped at weapon range. Click to lock it in.
- **Shots = the AP you had left when you cast it.** Casting spends all remaining AP and ends your turn. You need at least 1 AP to cast.
- Every enemy that moves or acts inside the cone draws a shot until the shots run out or your next turn starts.
- Each shot is the weapon''s normal single shot. Accuracy falls with distance through the game''s own distance factor; optionally add a further penalty for wide cones.
- **Re-shooting the same enemy** (user, 2026-10-26): allowed, but only after it has moved at least **2 tiles** since you last shot it, so it doesn''t take a full attack every square. Keep the 2-tile minimum as a tunable constant.
- **Spread fire first:** cycling between enemies always takes priority. While any enemy in the cone hasn''t been shot yet, an already-shot enemy moving again draws no fire; the shot is held for the others. Only when there''s no choice (every enemy in the cone has been shot, or it''s the only one) does it fire at the same enemy again, still subject to the 2-tile rule.
- Open question for testing: does a re-shot need movement, or can an already-shot enemy acting (shooting or casting) in the cone also draw a second shot? Proposal: only movement re-triggers; actions can only draw a first shot.

## Engine pieces (Code.dll)
- Zone: `AbilityEffectOverwatch` → `PartOverwatch`, Mode `MultipleTargetOverwatch` (doesn''t Stop after a shot), with HitsPerTarget set by the open question. Triggers come from `OverwatchController` (position changes, and abilities with `UsingInOverwatchArea: WillCauseAttack`).
- Drag cone: every pattern ability''s shape comes from `AbilityAoEPatternSettings.GetOrientedPattern(ability, casterNode, targetNode)`. The targeting preview (`AbilityPatternRange`) calls it as the cursor moves, and `AbilityEffectOverwatch.GetOverwatchArea` calls it when the zone is set, so preview and zone match. Harmony-patch it for this ability only to return `AoEPattern.Cone(angle(d), radius(d))` from the caster→cursor distance (`AoEPattern.Cone`/`CopyAndOverrideRadius`/`GridPatterns.ConstructPattern` build any size at runtime).
- Re-shot and priority rules: `PartOverwatch.TryTriggerAttack` only knows HitOnce (never again) or HitManyTimes (every trigger), so these need C#. Per overwatcher, keep each enemy''s cell at its last shot and its shot count. On a trigger, (1) skip if it was shot and has moved < 2 cells (grid distance) since; (2) skip if it was shot and any enemy currently inside the cone hasn''t been shot yet; otherwise fire and record. Position triggers arrive many times per move (`Entity.OnPositionChanged`), so the distance check also rate-limits them.
- Shot budget: no data component spends "all remaining AP", so in C#: on cast, read the caster''s remaining AP into the overwatch part (or a stacking buff via `AbilityEffectOverwatch.ApplyingBuffs`, whose rank shows the shots left), decrement on each `UnitOverwatchAttack`, then `Stop()` at 0. End the turn with `WarhammerEndTurn` (`clearMPInsteadOfEndingTurn: false`). Candidate to inspect: `WarhammerAbilityCostChangeContextValue`.
- Accuracy: `RuleCalculateHitChances.OnTriggerRanged` multiplies by `RuleCalculateAbilityDistanceFactor`. Extra overwatch penalty: a buff via `ApplyingBuffs` if a hit-chance modifier component exists, else a small patch on that rule for overwatch shots.
- C# home: the same assembly as Suppression''s movement-only rule (RTO-T8), either a UnityModManager mod (the user runs UMM: Brevity, ToyBox) or an Owlcat mod Scripts assembly.

## Acceptance
- Cone preview grows and shrinks with the cursor, and the locked zone matches it.
- Casting with 3 AP left fires at most 3 shots, then overwatch ends; it also ends at your next turn.
- Shots follow the weapon''s normal hit chance, which drops with distance.
- One enemy walking 6 tiles through the cone with 3 shots available draws shots at entry, after 2 tiles and after 4 tiles, not one per square.
- With two enemies in the cone, the second one gets shot before the first is shot again.', '2026-10-26T19:30:20Z');

Mode `MultipleTargetOverwatch` (doesn''t Stop after a shot) + HitsPerTarget `HitOnce` (PartOverwatch keeps m_AttackedUnits). Cone ~8 cells / 60°. Higher cost (all AP / ends turn) to balance.', '2026-10-26T18:25:57Z');

Engine facts (Code.dll, `OverwatchController`):
- `PartOverwatch` is zone-based: it fires at any enemy whose cells are in `OverwatchArea`. Triggers come from `HandleEntityPositionChanged` (every position update, likely many per move) and `HandleUnitCommandDidAct` (abilities with `UsingInOverwatchArea: WillCauseAttack`).
- Mode only controls whether the part `Stop()`s after a shot; `HitManyTimes` lets the same unit be shot again.

Design options (pick during implementation):
1. **Overwatch part plus a C# filter.** The ability targets an enemy (`CanTargetEnemies: true`). The zone is the target''s surroundings or the weapon''s range. A Harmony patch on `OverwatchController`/`PartOverwatch` for `SuppressionFire` parts (a) fires only at the chosen target, (b) ignores action triggers so only movement counts, and (c) fires once per move command (attack-of-opportunity style) rather than once per position update.
2. **Debuff on the target.** A "Suppressed" buff that triggers on its owner''s movement and makes the caster shoot it. Check Code.dll for a real movement-trigger component first; the pasted guide''s `AddInitiatorUnitMovementTrigger` doesn''t exist. Also gives a visible status icon on the enemy.
Option 2 fits better if a movement-trigger component exists, because the target can see it''s pinned. Either way some C# is likely; it shares that code with RTO-T12.
Cost: ends the turn. The AI should see the suppressed state and prefer not to move (check whether it reads buffs or only `PartOverwatch.OverwatchArea` threats).

Acceptance: the suppressed enemy that shoots without moving takes no fire; when it moves it takes one shot per move; other enemies moving nearby take none; ends at your next turn.

## Cosmetic suppressive fire (user, 2026-10-26)
During the suppressed enemy''s turn, the hero keeps firing at it as a show of force: shooting animation, muzzle flash, sound and projectiles only. **No damage, no hit rolls, no ammo, no combat-log lines, and every shot visibly misses.** It sells "if you move, you get hit". When the enemy actually moves, the real attack of opportunity (normal hit chance and damage) replaces the cosmetic fire for that shot.

Engine pieces (Code.dll):
- `Kingmaker.Controllers.Projectiles.ProjectileLauncher(BlueprintProjectile, MechanicEntity launcher, TargetWrapper target)` → `.Launch()`. Target a `TargetWrapper` *point*, not the unit: a miss point beside or above the enemy, plus a little random scatter, so it can''t intersect. Don''t attach `OnHitRule`/`AttackResult`, so nothing is applied. `ProjectileLauncher` also has `m_MisdirectionOffset`, used for misses; check how real misses use it so ours look the same.
- Projectile and FX: from the hero''s weapon (its first weapon ability''s `BlueprintAbilityFXSettings` / projectile blueprint), so a lasgun shows las bolts and a stubber shows rounds.
- Animation: play the weapon''s shoot animation on the hero (`Kingmaker.Visual.Animation.Kingmaker.UnitAnimationActionHandle`; find how `UnitUseAbility` starts the shoot animation) without running a command, so turn order and AP aren''t touched.
- Timing: a runner (controller tick or coroutine) active while the suppressed enemy''s turn is running (`ITurnStartHandler` / turn end). Fire a burst every ~1.5–2.5 s with jitter. Stop when the turn ends, the hero or target dies, the target leaves line of sight, or suppression ends; pause while the real AoO shot plays.
- Rules: purely visual, so skip it in multiplayer or keep it local-only (no game-state change, safe for saves and the state hasher).

Acceptance (addition): during the suppressed enemy''s turn the hero fires visible bursts that always miss and change nothing (HP, ammo, log); moving still triggers the real attack of opportunity.', '2026-10-26T19:32:20Z');

Mode `Overwatch`, HitOnce, Ray ~12 cells (cut `VeteranOverwatchPatternLine12` used Ray radius 12). Good for sniper/las weapons; shows whether a line reads better than a cone in play.', '2026-10-26T18:26:01Z');

Cause: Clouds(SceneryData data, Random random = null) and Weather(SceneryData data, Random random = null) store ''random ?? new Random()'' in the field, but the picture draw ''random.Next(1, c.Textures.Count + 1)'' (Clouds, line 237) and the direction draw ''switch (random.Next(1, 5))'' (Weather, line 153) inside the constructors use the parameter, which is null when unseeded. Percent() uses the field, so only those two draws break.

Fix: use this.random in both constructors (or rename the parameter). Test: Core test constructing Clouds and Weather with no Random must not throw.', '2026-10-26T19:37:06Z');

